            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the contribution Howard but it's going to take a lot of convincing to get this one in. PSM has always been designed to be simple and act as a systemd wrapper. It's always been a conscious decision to not put state machine logic in it and instead trust the user. If they want to power off, run the systemd target to power off. If they want to power on, run that target. Any state \"rules\" has been left to the external interfaces if they so desire (for example I believe the OpenBMC GUI has some rules similar to what you have in this code)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "@patrick@stwcx.xyz\nWould it make more sense to modify the code in mfg-tool instead?\n\nJira: YV4T1M-1898"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "After today's discussion, this feature will not be applied."
                },
                {
                    "file": "chassis_state_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 598,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why would cycle not be allowed when we are already on?  A cycle should be an off + on."
                },
                {
                    "file": "chassis_state_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 598,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "It checks the state of chassis-power-cycle@{}.service, which is active only during the BMC power cycle process and becomes inactive once the process is finished.\n\nIn the power-on state, the system verifies if the power-on or power-cycle service is still active. If so, any incoming command is denied until the service completes."
                },
                {
                    "file": "chassis_state_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 607,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why is a cycle not allowed in off state?  Wouldn't this just turn into an 'on'?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "chassis_state_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 607,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "It checks the state of chassis-power-cycle@{}.service, which is active only during the BMC power cycle process and becomes inactive once the process is finished.\n\nIn the power-off state, the system verifies if the power-off or power-cycle service is still active. If so, any incoming command is denied until the service completes."
                },
                {
                    "file": "chassis_state_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 616,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "We absolutely want to be able to abort / stop a power operation in progress.  This is not right."
                },
                {
                    "file": "chassis_state_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 616,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "Sure, I will fix it."
                }
            ],
